; ======================================================================
; dma.pi4 - THE SoC's 2D BLITTER. Pixels moved by hardware, not by the
;           CPU, using the legacy DMA engine's 2D mode at $FE007000.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; This file exists because of one measured number.
;
;   1920 x 1080 x 32bpp, on this board, 2026-08-26, from
;   RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4NeonLiar.pi4:
;
;                           MMU off      MMU on, DRAM cacheable
;       clear, stores only  356.9 ms          266.4 ms
;       blend, best of two 2718.8 ms         2172.0 ms
;
;   against a 25 ms frame budget. 266.4 ms to store 8.29 MB is 48
;   cycles per byte - 194 cycles to write one four-byte pixel, where a
;   store loop should cost one or two. Turning the caches on bought
;   1.3x and could not buy more, because a compositor STREAMS: every
;   line is touched once per frame, so there is no reuse for a cache to
;   exploit (the same argument is written up at damage.pi4's head).
;
; The cleanest way around a slow CPU loop is to not use the CPU. This
; file is that: a hardware rectangle fill and a hardware rectangle copy
; that the ARM starts and then does not participate in.
;
; ======================================================================
; USE - in the MAIN file. Libraries do not include libraries (project
; ruling, 2026-07-30, house rule 10):
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4"
;
; THIS FILE INCLUDES NOTHING. It reads CNTPCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0
; directly for its timeouts - the same choice mailbox.pi4 made and for
; the same reason (mailbox.pi4:33-40) - so it has no dependency to
; declare, no include order to get wrong, and can be the first thing a
; program calls. Its counter helpers are DmaTicks() and DmaCounterHz()
; rather than Ticks() and TickHz() precisely because timer.pi4 owns
; those two names and reads the same two registers.
;
; The shortest correct sequence:
;
;     DmaSetScratch($700000, 512)              ; 256-byte aligned, low 1 GiB
;     DmaSetBounds(DisplayBase(), DisplaySize()) ; the ONLY writable window
;     If DmaInit() = 0
;       ... DmaErrorText() says why ...
;     EndIf
;     DmaFill32(DisplayBase(), DisplayPitch(), 1920, 1080, colour)
;
; ======================================================================
; THREE HAZARDS. EACH ONE LOOKS LIKE ONE OF THE OTHERS AT THE BENCH.
; ======================================================================
;
; HAZARD 1 - THE LEGACY ENGINES CANNOT SEE ABOVE ARM PHYSICAL
;            $40000000, AND THAT IS NOT THE $C0000000 LIMIT EVERYONE
;            QUOTES.
;
;   Two different caps are in circulation on this board and they are
;   not the same cap:
;
;     * $C0000000 is the PCIe controller's inbound window
;       (pcie.pi4:472, #PCIE_DMA_LIMIT, from bcm2711.dtsi's pcie node).
;       PcieDmaOk() is the check, and it is the right check for xHCI
;       and for anything else hanging off the root complex.
;
;     * $40000000 - ONE GIGABYTE - is what THIS engine can reach, and
;       it is three quarters tighter. Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:45:
;
;           dma-ranges = <0xc0000000  0x0 0x00000000  0x40000000>;
;
;       Read as <bus, parent-hi, parent-lo, size>: bus $C0000000 is ARM
;       physical $00000000 for $40000000 bytes, and there is no second
;       range. The vendor PDF says the same thing from the hardware
;       side - the ENABLE register's PAGE field
;       (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:4994-5002) exists to "set the 1G SDRAM
;       ram page that the 30-bit DMA engines (DMA0-6) will access when
;       addressing the 1G uncached range C000_0000->ffff_ffff", which
;       is only necessary because the engines are THIRTY-BIT.
;
;   So a buffer at $80000000 is DMA-poison for this engine even though
;   PcieDmaOk() would wave it through. DmaAddrOk() is the check that
;   belongs to this file, and every entry point calls it.
;
;   THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH THE PAGE FIELD. Moving the
;   window would move it for DMA0-6 as a whole, including any channel
;   the firmware is using, and the failure would land somewhere with no
;   visible connection to graphics. If a buffer above 1 GiB has to be
;   reached, the answer is a DMA4 channel (see the last section), not a
;   page flip behind the firmware's back.
;
;   THE FRAMEBUFFER IS SAFE. display.pi4:2053 computes
;   dsp_base = bus & $3FFFFFFF, so the surface is in the low gigabyte
;   by construction and this engine can always reach it.
;
; HAZARD 2 - THE CACHES ARE ON NOW, AND A MISSING CLEAN LOOKS EXACTLY
;            LIKE HAZARD 1.
;
;   As of 2026-08-26 DRAM maps Normal Write-Back Cacheable while a
;   payload has the MMU on (#MMU_DRAM_ATTR = $711 in mmu.pi4). So:
;
;     * a control block the CPU just wrote is in a dirty cache line and
;       the engine reads DRAM. It must be CLEANED first.
;     * a fill pattern the CPU just wrote is in the same position.
;     * pixels the engine just wrote are in DRAM and the CPU may hold
;       stale lines over them. They must be INVALIDATED first.
;
;   This file cleans its own control block and its own pattern on every
;   transfer - the caller cannot be expected to know they exist. It
;   does NOT clean the caller's source rectangle or invalidate the
;   caller's destination, because it does not know what else the caller
;   has touched or is about to touch; DmaCacheRange() is public for
;   exactly that, and the discipline is stated at each entry point.
;
;   The reason to labour this: a buffer above the 1 GiB cap produces a
;   device that accepts everything and completes nothing, and a missing
;   cache clean produces a transfer that copies stale bytes. At the
;   bench, with a screen full of wrong pixels, those are hard to tell
;   apart. CHECK THE ADDRESS FIRST - it is a comparison, and the cache
;   question is not. mmu.pi4:310-319 makes the same point.
;
; HAZARD 3 - A RUNAWAY DESTINATION IS THE ONE FAULT THAT NEEDS A POWER
;            CYCLE.
;
;   Nothing here can damage hardware. But this engine writes to
;   PHYSICAL addresses with no MMU in its path, so a wrong destination
;   is a bus master scribbling over the monitor's code, a parked core's
;   spin table, or a payload - and the symptom is a board that stops
;   answering rather than a bad picture.
;
;   THEREFORE DmaSetBounds() IS MANDATORY AND EVERY TRANSFER IS
;   REFUSED UNTIL IT HAS BEEN CALLED. The caller declares one window
;   that this file may write into - normally the framebuffer - and any
;   rectangle whose last byte falls outside it is refused with
;   #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS before a single register is written.
;
;   That is stricter than most libraries here are, and it is on
;   purpose. genet.pi4 can afford to check only the size of a region
;   it was handed because the device writes inside a ring it owns. A
;   blitter takes a fresh destination on every call, from arithmetic
;   the caller did, and an off-by-one in that arithmetic is a store
;   into whatever follows the surface.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY THE LEGACY ENGINE AND NOT V3D
; ======================================================================
; From "Raspberry Pi 4/NeonArcade port plan 2026-08-26.md" section 9.3,
; which is agreeing with Datasheets/pi4/v3d/v3d-feasibility.md:527-543:
; the 2D mode of the legacy DMA controller is "the best value in the
; whole note... the only item here that does not depend on a single
; line of reverse engineering". Every register, bit and field this file
; touches is in the vendor PDF, cited by line below.
;
; It is also squarely BELOW the graphics seam. This file knows about
; bytes, rows, pitches and one pixel size; it does not know what a
; window is, does not clip, does not blend, and does not include
; display.pi4. A compositor calls it; it never calls a compositor.
;
; WHAT IT CANNOT DO, said plainly so nobody plans around a capability
; that is not here: IT DOES NOT BLEND. It is a copy engine. It
; accelerates opaque fills, rectangle copies, scrolling, and pushing a
; shadow buffer to the framebuffer. The alpha composite still costs
; what it costs, and the way to make that cheaper is to do less of it
; (damage.pi4) or to do it on the QPUs (V3D), not here.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE HARDWARE, AND WHERE EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE COMES FROM
; ======================================================================
; Source of truth: Datasheets/bcm2711-peripherals.pdf, chapter 4 "DMA
; Controller", and the extracted bcm2711-peripherals.txt beside it.
; Line numbers below are the .txt; PDF page numbers are in the
; extracted footers, chapter 4 running from document page 30 to 64.
;
;   :2292-2293  "DMA Channel 0 is located at the address of 0x7e007000,
;               Channel 1 at 0x7e007100 ... adjacent DMA Channels are
;               offset by 0x100."
;   :2293-2294  Channel 15 is at 0x7ee05000 and is "exclusively used by
;               the VPU". This file will not go near it.
;   :2351-2380  Table 34, the Control Block: eight 32-bit words, in the
;               order TI, SOURCE_AD, DEST_AD, TXFR_LEN, STRIDE,
;               NEXTCONBK, and two reserved words that must be zero.
;   :2342-2345  Only CS, CONBLK_AD and DEBUG are directly writeable.
;               Everything else is loaded from the CB.
;   :2442-2450  Start by writing the CB address into CONBLK_AD and then
;               setting ACTIVE. The engine stops and clears ACTIVE when
;               it loads a NEXTCONBK of zero.
;   :3023-3210  Table 38, the CS register, bit by bit.
;   :3226-3234  CONBLK_AD. "The address must be 256-bit aligned, so the
;               bottom 5 bits of the address must be zero."
;   :3241-3410  Table 40, the TI register, bit by bit.
;   :3411-3490  Table 43, TXFR_LEN: YLENGTH in 29:16, XLENGTH in 15:0.
;   :3491-3515  Table 44, STRIDE: D_STRIDE in 31:16, S_STRIDE in 15:0,
;               both SIGNED 16-bit.
;   :3537-3620  Table 46, DEBUG, including the three sticky error bits.
;   :3017-3021  INT_STATUS at 0xfe0, ENABLE at 0xff0.
;   :4970-5100  Table 62, ENABLE: PAGELITE 31:28, PAGE 27:24, EN14..EN0
;               in 14:0, all reset to 1.
;   :5230-5246  Section 4.3, AXI Bursts. Channel 0 and channel 15 are
;               the two with an external 128-bit 8-word read FIFO,
;               which is what makes memory-to-memory efficient.
;   :5247-5258  Section 4.4, Error Handling.
;   :5262-5272  Section 4.5, the LITE engines: channels 7-10 have NO 2D
;               MODE AT ALL - "The TDMODE, S_STRIDE, D_STRIDE and
;               YLENGTH registers will all be removed. Setting these
;               registers will have no effect." That is why this file
;               refuses channels 7-10 rather than quietly producing a
;               linear transfer of the wrong length.
;   :5277-5283  Section 4.6, the DMA4 engines: channels 11-14, 40 address
;               bits, write bursts, and they "bypass the paging
;               registers". The last section of this header says what
;               that would buy and why it is not what this file is.
;
; WHICH CHANNELS ARE FREE, and the citation is the device tree because
; the PDF does not say. Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:106:
;
;     brcm,dma-channel-mask = <0x07f5>;
;
; $07F5 is 0000 0111 1111 0101 - channels 0, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10.
; Bits 1 and 3 are clear, so CHANNELS 1 AND 3 BELONG TO THE FIRMWARE
; and nothing here may take them. Of what is left, only 0, 2, 4, 5 and
; 6 are full engines with 2D mode; 7-10 are LITE. Channel 0 is the
; default here because it is one of the two with the read FIFO
; (:5236-5238).
;
; THE DEFAULT IS A CHOICE AND IT COULD BE WRONG. The mask above is what
; the Linux device tree claims, not what the running firmware has
; promised. The firmware will also answer the question directly, via
; mailbox tag $00060001 GET_DMA_CHANNELS, and a program that has
; mailbox.pi4 in its include list should ASK and then call
; DmaSetChannel() with a channel the firmware admits to. This file
; cannot ask; it has no mailbox and, by house rule 10, may not acquire
; one. RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DmaProbe.pi4 does ask, and
; prints both answers side by side.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE ONE THING THE VENDOR PDF LEAVES AMBIGUOUS, AND WHAT THIS FILE
; DOES ABOUT IT
; ======================================================================
; Is YLENGTH the NUMBER of rows, or the number of rows MINUS ONE?
;
; The DMA4 table is explicit (:4778-4779): "In 2D mode a value of 0
; will result in a single XLENGTH transfer and a value of 1 will result
; in 2 XLENGTH transfers." That is N-1.
;
; The LEGACY table, Table 43 at :3477-3480, describes the same field in
; the same words - "indicating how many xlength transfers are
; performed" - AND OMITS THAT SENTENCE ENTIRELY. So the legacy engine
; is either N or N-1 and the document does not say which.
;
; This is not a difference that can be split. Guess N when the part
; wants N-1 and every fill writes one row too many, off the bottom of
; the rectangle and, on the last rectangle, off the end of the surface.
; Guess N-1 when the part wants N and every fill leaves the bottom row
; of every rectangle unpainted, which reads as a tearing artefact and
; gets blamed on the pitch.
;
; SO THIS FILE MEASURES IT. DmaInit() runs a 2-row, 8-byte 2D transfer
; into the scratch block, counts how many rows actually changed, and
; sets an internal bias of 0 or 1 that every later transfer applies.
; It also checks a 64-byte guard band after the calibration target and
; refuses to initialise if anything landed in it. DmaYLengthBias()
; reports what was found, so the answer gets written down once and for
; all the first time this runs on silicon.
;
; The calibration is cheap - 16 bytes moved - and it doubles as proof
; that 2D mode works at all on the chosen channel BEFORE anything is
; aimed at the framebuffer. That ordering is the point.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY WAIT_FOR_OUTSTANDING_WRITES AND NOT WAIT_RESP
; ======================================================================
; Two bits promise something about writes having landed and they cost
; very different amounts.
;
;   TI bit 3, WAIT_RESP (:3375-3388), makes the engine wait for the AXI
;   write response FOR EACH WRITE. That serialises the whole transfer
;   on bus round trips and throws away the reason for using DMA.
;
;   CS bit 28, WAIT_FOR_OUTSTANDING_WRITES (:3050-3070), keeps a tally
;   instead: writes stream out freely, and at the very end of the
;   transfer the engine defers clearing ACTIVE and setting END until
;   the last write response has come back.
;
; The second is what a blitter wants: full speed, and a completion flag
; that means the pixels are actually in memory rather than in flight.
; This file sets CS.WAIT_FOR_OUTSTANDING_WRITES on every start and
; leaves TI.WAIT_RESP clear. The PDF notes the engine will pause if it
; has more than 13 outstanding writes, which is a throttle, not a
; correctness problem.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
; ======================================================================
;   * NO INTERRUPTS. TI.INTEN stays clear and every transfer is
;     completion-polled. There is no interrupt controller layer in this
;     tree for a library to hook, and a blocking blitter that is ten
;     times faster than the CPU loop is already the whole win. The
;     asynchronous door is left open on purpose: DmaBusy() and
;     DmaWait() are public, so an overlapped variant is a small
;     addition and not a rewrite.
;   * NO CHAINED CONTROL BLOCKS. One CB per call, NEXTCONBK zero. A
;     chain is how a scatter of rectangles becomes one submission, and
;     that belongs with the damage tracker that knows what the
;     rectangles are - not here, and not before there is a caller.
;   * NO PERIPHERAL PACING. PERMAP stays 0, "continuous un-paced
;     transfer" (:3285-3292). This file moves memory to memory.
;   * NO BLEND. Said above; said again because it is the capability
;     people assume a blitter has.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE NEXT RUNG, NAMED SO IT DOES NOT HAVE TO BE REDISCOVERED
; ======================================================================
; The DMA4 engines - channels 11 to 14, at $FE007B00 and 0x100 apart -
; are strictly better hardware for this job on three counts, all from
; :5277-5283 and the register map at :2836-2900:
;
;   1. FORTY ADDRESS BITS. No 1 GiB cap, no bus alias, no PAGE field.
;      A shadow buffer could live anywhere in the 4 GiB.
;   2. WRITE BURSTS. The legacy engines do not do them except as
;      2-beat wide writes; DMA4 does, and a fill is all writes.
;   3. UNCOUPLED READ AND WRITE paths.
;
; What it costs is a second control-block format: DMA4's CB is eight
; words in the order TI, SRC, SRCI, DEST, DESTI, LEN, NEXT_CB, DEBUG2,
; with the top address bits and the per-side burst and width fields
; living in the SRCI and DESTI words (:2836-2900, and the field tables
; at :4400-4800). Channel 11 is taken - the firmware uses it for VCHIQ,
; per the comment at rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi:168 - so
; 12, 13 and 14 are the candidates.
;
; That is a separate day's work with its own bring-up, and it is not
; what "1-2 days, vendor-documented, no reverse engineering" was
; scoped for. It is written down here so the choice is a choice.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  ADDRESSES AND REGISTER OFFSETS
; ======================================================================

; Bus $7E007000 -> ARM $FE007000 through the soc node's ranges,
; bcm2711.dtsi:40. The node itself is bcm2711.dtsi:79-81,
; dma-controller@7e007000 with reg = <0x7e007000 0xb00>. Note the 0xb00
; span: the device tree stops before the DMA4 channels because it
; describes them as a separate node.
#DMA_BASE     = $FE007000
#DMA_CHAN_STEP = $100          ; :2292-2293

; Per-channel registers, all offsets from the channel base. :2460-2503
#DMA_CS        = $00
#DMA_CONBLK_AD = $04
#DMA_TI        = $08
#DMA_SOURCE_AD = $0C
#DMA_DEST_AD   = $10
#DMA_TXFR_LEN  = $14
#DMA_STRIDE    = $18
#DMA_NEXTCONBK = $1C
#DMA_DEBUG     = $20

; Global registers, offsets from #DMA_BASE. :3017-3021
#DMA_INT_STATUS = $FE0
#DMA_ENABLE     = $FF0

; CS bits. Table 38, :3023-3210.
#DMA_CS_RESET       = $80000000   ; bit 31, W1SC, :3035-3040
#DMA_CS_ABORT       = $40000000   ; bit 30, W1SC, :3042-3048
#DMA_CS_DISDEBUG    = $20000000   ; bit 29,       :3029-3034
#DMA_CS_WAIT_WRITES = $10000000   ; bit 28,       :3050-3070
#DMA_CS_ERROR       = $00000100   ; bit  8, RO,   :3113-3122
#DMA_CS_WAITING     = $00000040   ; bit  6, RO,   :3130-3140
#DMA_CS_DREQ_STOPS  = $00000020   ; bit  5, RO,   :3142-3152
#DMA_CS_PAUSED      = $00000010   ; bit  4, RO,   :3154-3168
#DMA_CS_DREQ        = $00000008   ; bit  3, RO,   :3172-3184
#DMA_CS_INT         = $00000004   ; bit  2, W1C,  :3185-3193
#DMA_CS_END         = $00000002   ; bit  1, W1C,  :3194-3200
#DMA_CS_ACTIVE      = $00000001   ; bit  0, RW,   :3201-3210

; PRIORITY is 19:16 and PANIC_PRIORITY is 23:20, :3090-3112. Zero is
; the LOWEST priority, and a blitter competing with the display
; controller's own fetches should not be at the bottom of the arbiter.
; 8 of a possible 15 is a deliberate middle: high enough that a
; full-surface fill is not starved, low enough that it cannot outrank a
; peripheral that panics because its FIFO is about to underrun.
#DMA_PRIORITY_SHIFT       = 16
#DMA_PANIC_PRIORITY_SHIFT = 20
#DMA_DEFAULT_PRIORITY     = 8

; TI bits. Table 40, :3241-3410.
#DMA_TI_NO_WIDE_BURSTS = $04000000  ; bit 26,     :3267-3275
#DMA_TI_WAITS_SHIFT    = 21         ; bits 25:21, :3276-3284
#DMA_TI_PERMAP_SHIFT   = 16         ; bits 20:16, :3285-3293
#DMA_TI_BURST_SHIFT    = 12         ; bits 15:12, :3294-3302
#DMA_TI_SRC_IGNORE     = $00000800  ; bit 11,     :3303-3311
#DMA_TI_SRC_DREQ       = $00000400  ; bit 10,     :3312-3319
#DMA_TI_SRC_WIDTH      = $00000200  ; bit  9,     :3320-3326
#DMA_TI_SRC_INC        = $00000100  ; bit  8,     :3327-3335
#DMA_TI_DEST_IGNORE    = $00000080  ; bit  7,     :3336-3342
#DMA_TI_DEST_DREQ      = $00000040  ; bit  6,     :3343-3349
#DMA_TI_DEST_WIDTH     = $00000020  ; bit  5,     :3350-3356
#DMA_TI_DEST_INC       = $00000010  ; bit  4,     :3367-3374
#DMA_TI_WAIT_RESP      = $00000008  ; bit  3,     :3375-3388
#DMA_TI_TDMODE         = $00000002  ; bit  1,     :3391-3400
#DMA_TI_INTEN          = $00000001  ; bit  0,     :3401-3410

; DEBUG bits. Table 46, :3537-3620. The three at the bottom are the
; sticky error latches and all three are write-1-to-clear.
#DMA_DEBUG_LITE           = $10000000  ; bit 28, RO
#DMA_DEBUG_READ_ERROR     = $00000004  ; bit  2, W1C, :3604-3610
#DMA_DEBUG_FIFO_ERROR     = $00000002  ; bit  1, W1C, :3611-3616
#DMA_DEBUG_READ_LAST_ERR  = $00000001  ; bit  0, W1C, :3617-3622
#DMA_DEBUG_ERROR_MASK     = $00000007  ; all three, cleared together

; Field limits, from the register widths themselves.
;   XLENGTH is 15:0        -> at most 65535 bytes in one row
;   YLENGTH is 29:16       -> at most 16383 rows
;   D_STRIDE / S_STRIDE    -> SIGNED 16-bit, so -32768 .. +32767
; A 1920-pixel 32bpp row is 7680 bytes and a 1080-row surface is well
; inside all three, but a caller with a wider surface is not, and the
; failure of an overflowed XLENGTH is a fill of the wrong width rather
; than a refusal. Hence the checks.
#DMA_MAX_ROW_BYTES = 65535
#DMA_MAX_ROWS      = 16383
#DMA_STRIDE_MAX    =  32767
; Nothing in this file produces a negative stride, because DmaGeomOk()
; refuses a pitch smaller than the row - see the note there on why a
; case with no caller is a case with an unexercised sign bug. The
; constant is here because the FIELD is signed and the next person to
; add a bottom-up surface or an overlapping-row layout needs to know
; that the hardware allows it and this file simply does not use it.
#DMA_STRIDE_MIN    = -32768

; THE REACH OF THE 30-BIT ENGINES. bcm2711.dtsi:45 and the PAGE field
; at bcm2711-peripherals.txt:4994-5002. See HAZARD 1 in the header.
#DMA_LIMIT     = $40000000
#DMA_BUS_ALIAS = $C0000000     ; the uncached view the engines address
#DMA_BUS_MASK  = $3FFFFFFF     ; the 30 bits that are actually decoded

; The Cortex-A72 cache line, the same constant display.pi4 and
; genet.pi4 use.
#DMA_CACHE_LINE = 64

; The control block is eight words and must start on a 32-byte
; boundary, :3232-3234.
;
; #DMA_CB_ALIGN IS NOT WHAT DmaSetScratch() CHECKS ANY MORE - it checks
; #DMA_SCRATCH_ALIGN, which is 256 and therefore satisfies this as a
; side effect. The constant stays because it is the hardware's actual
; requirement for CONBLK_AD and the next person to lay out the scratch
; block differently needs to know that 32 is the real floor and 256 is
; a choice the fill pattern forced.
#DMA_CB_BYTES  = 32
#DMA_CB_ALIGN  = 32

; ======================================================================
;  THE SCRATCH BLOCK - what it is for and why the caller owns it
; ======================================================================
;  This file needs a small piece of memory that BOTH the CPU and the
;  DMA engine can see: somewhere to build the control block, somewhere
;  to keep the repeating pattern a solid fill reads from, and a target
;  for the one-off calibration described in the header.
;
;  IT IS NOT A `Global Dim` HERE, for genet.pi4's reason
;  (genet.pi4:2169-2176) and one more of its own:
;
;    * BSS is imposed on every program that so much as includes this
;      file, and this backend does not dead-strip - math.pi4:127-132
;      records that including a library costs its whole size.
;
;    * AND THE ADDRESS WOULD BE WRONG HALF THE TIME. A payload's BSS
;      sits 512 KiB above its load address (#A64_BSS_GAP in
;      A64Assembler.pbi), so a program loaded into the HIGH window at
;      $40000000 and upward - which memory.pi4 recommends for anything
;      large - would have its BSS above the 1 GiB cap and every
;      transfer would fail. A caller who is told to supply the memory
;      is a caller who has been made to think about where it is.
;
;  THE LAYOUT, all offsets from the base the caller gives:
;
;      +0   .. +31    the control block. 32 bytes, 32-byte aligned.
;      +32  .. +255   reserved, and nothing writes here today. It is
;                     claimed because the chained multi-rectangle
;                     submission the damage tracker will eventually
;                     want needs a SECOND control block, and enlarging
;                     the required size later would silently break
;                     every caller that had sized its block exactly.
;      +256 .. +511   the fill pattern. TWO HUNDRED AND FIFTY-SIX bytes
;                     of the colour repeated, read over and over with
;                     SRC_INC clear. The size is not arbitrary and the
;                     next section is entirely about why.
;
;  THE CALIBRATION WRITES NOTHING AT ALL. It is a reads-only transfer
;  with TI.DEST_IGNORE set, and it counts rows by reading back the
;  engine's own SOURCE_AD register afterwards. That is a deliberate
;  design constraint and DmaCalibrate() explains why the obvious
;  alternative - fill a target and count what changed - was rejected.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHY THE PATTERN IS 256 BYTES. THIS WAS A BUG, IT REACHED SILICON,
;  AND THE FIX IS THE SIZE OF THIS NUMBER.
; ======================================================================
;  IT WAS 32 BYTES UNTIL 2026-08-26 AND THAT WAS WRONG. The reasoning
;  that produced 32 is left here because it is exactly the reasoning
;  that has to be replaced rather than patched:
;
;    "The engine reads 128 bits at a time (TI.SRC_WIDTH) and does not
;     advance the source (TI.SRC_INC clear), so it re-reads the same
;     wide word forever. 16 bytes covers a 128-bit read; the PDF's
;     description of SRC_INC says the address 'will increment by 4, if
;     SRC_WIDTH=0 else by 32' (:3327-3335), so the internal register is
;     256 bits; 32 bytes covers both readings."
;
;  Every sentence of that is true and the conclusion is still wrong,
;  because it reasons about the WIDTH of one access and the transfer is
;  not made of one access. IT IS MADE OF BURSTS, AND A BURST READS
;  CONSECUTIVE ADDRESSES. TI.SRC_INC governs whether the engine's
;  source POINTER advances between transfers; it does not and cannot
;  make the beats within a single AXI burst target one address.
;
;  ---- WHAT THE BENCH SAW, 2026-08-26 -----------------------------
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DmaGate.pi4, 59 of 60 cases
;  agreeing with the CPU, and one group A case not:
;
;    FAIL fill w=1024 h=8 dp=2048 off=0
;         first bad byte at +32:  cpu $0D  dma $00   INSIDE the rectangle
;
;  ---- THE ONE FACT THAT DECIDES IT -------------------------------
;  THE GATE SEEDS BOTH BUFFERS WITH $3C BEFORE EVERY CASE, NOT WITH
;  ZERO, and it does that precisely so this question can be answered.
;  So the byte at +32 is $00 and not $3C, which means THE ENGINE WROTE
;  THERE. It did not stop early, it did not fall short, and it was not
;  refused. It wrote, and what it wrote was zero.
;
;  That single comparison eliminates the two suspects a short fill
;  normally has:
;
;    * A TRUNCATED XLENGTH would leave the tail of the row untouched,
;      so the bench would have read $3C. It read $00. Not this.
;      (And 1024 is $400, nowhere near the 16-bit field's edge.)
;    * A WRONG D_STRIDE puts correct data in the wrong PLACE. The
;      damage here is 32 bytes into row ZERO, before any stride has
;      been applied even once. Not this either - and the bracketing
;      case in group A2 with pitch = rowBytes proves it independently
;      by removing the stride from the picture altogether.
;
;  What is left has to explain where ZEROS came from, and there is only
;  one source of data in a fill: the pattern. The engine read past the
;  end of a 32-byte pattern into the reserved area behind it, which
;  nothing had written, and faithfully stored what it found.
;
;  The boundary fits the mechanism. Channel 0 is one of the two "fitted
;  with an external 128-bit 8 word read FIFO... This enables efficient
;  memory to memory transfers to be performed" (:5236-5238). Eight
;  words of 128 bits is 128 BYTES. Bursts are "only produced for
;  specific conditions" (:3298-3302), and a long row is evidently one
;  of them: every case at 256 bytes a row and below passed, the single
;  case at 1024 did not, and the good data stopped at exactly the
;  length of the pattern rather than at any multiple of the row, the
;  pitch or the stride.
;
;  ---- THE FIX, AND WHY THIS SIZE AND NOT 128 ---------------------
;  The source region must be at least as large as the largest read
;  burst the engine can issue. Section 4.3 opens with the ceiling:
;  "The DMA supports bursts under specific conditions. Up to 16 beat
;  bursts can be accommodated." (:5231). Sixteen beats of 128 bits is
;  256 BYTES.
;
;  So 256, not 128. 128 would cover channel 0's FIFO and would be
;  sized to a mechanism rather than to a limit - and the whole reason
;  this bug exists is that 32 was sized to a mechanism. The limit is
;  the thing to size to, it is stated in one sentence in the vendor
;  PDF, and the cost of being generous is 224 bytes of the caller's
;  scratch.
;
;  It also means DmaSetBurst() cannot reintroduce the fault at any
;  legal value: the field is four bits, its maximum is 15, and 16 beats
;  is already covered.
;
;  ---- WHY THE COPY PATH WAS NEVER AFFECTED -----------------------
;  Because SRC_INC is SET there, so the source is a real region that
;  the caller sized, and reading forward is the entire point. Group C
;  passed on all fourteen cases including the 1024-byte rows, which is
;  the control that isolates this to the fill.
; ======================================================================
#DMA_SCRATCH_CB       = 0
#DMA_SCRATCH_RESERVED = 32
#DMA_SCRATCH_PATTERN  = 256
#DMA_SCRATCH_BYTES    = 512    ; the minimum, and what to ask for
#DMA_PATTERN_BYTES    = 256

; The scratch base must be 256-byte aligned, not merely 32. The control
; block needs 32 (CONBLK_AD is 256-BIT aligned, :3232-3234); the
; PATTERN wants 256 so that the one maximal burst the engine may issue
; from its base cannot straddle a 4 KiB boundary, which is the one
; place an AXI burst is architecturally required to break. Asking for
; the larger of the two once is simpler than carrying two rules.
#DMA_SCRATCH_ALIGN = 256

; The calibration transfer. FOUR bytes in one row, ONE in the YLENGTH
; field, 32-bit reads and no burst, so the source address advances by
; exactly four per row performed and the answer is unambiguous.
#DMA_CALIB_XLEN = 4
#DMA_CALIB_YLEN = 1


; ======================================================================
;  ERROR CODES
;
;  Negative, so a procedure that returns a count can return an error in
;  the same slot and a caller can test for less than zero. DmaError()
;  holds the last one and DmaErrorText() puts it in words.
; ======================================================================
#DMA_ERR_NONE      =  0
#DMA_ERR_NOSCRATCH = -1   ; DmaSetScratch() was never called, or refused
#DMA_ERR_ALIGN     = -2   ; the scratch block is not 32-byte aligned
#DMA_ERR_SMALL     = -3   ; the scratch block is shorter than the minimum
#DMA_ERR_REACH     = -4   ; an address is at or above the 1 GiB cap
#DMA_ERR_CHANNEL   = -5   ; not a channel this file may drive
#DMA_ERR_LITE      = -6   ; a LITE channel was asked for, and it has no 2D
#DMA_ERR_DISABLED  = -7   ; the channel's bit in the global ENABLE is clear
#DMA_ERR_BUSY      = -8   ; the channel was already ACTIVE when we arrived
#DMA_ERR_TIMEOUT   = -9   ; the transfer did not finish inside the deadline
#DMA_ERR_HW        = -10  ; CS.ERROR, or a sticky bit in DEBUG
#DMA_ERR_ARGS      = -11  ; width, height, pitch or colour out of range
#DMA_ERR_STRIDE    = -12  ; the stride does not fit in a signed 16-bit field
#DMA_ERR_BOUNDS    = -13  ; the destination is outside the declared window
#DMA_ERR_NOBOUNDS  = -14  ; DmaSetBounds() was never called
#DMA_ERR_NOTREADY  = -15  ; DmaInit() has not run, or refused
#DMA_ERR_CALIB     = -16  ; the 2D calibration produced a result that is
                          ; neither of the two readings of YLENGTH
#DMA_ERR_OVERLAP   = -17  ; a copy whose source and destination overlap
#DMA_ERR_ROWLIMIT  = -18  ; a fill row longer than #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES
#DMA_ERR_BURST     = -19  ; a burst length at or above the bus-locking one


; ======================================================================
;  THE FILL ROW LIMIT, AND THE ONE LINE TO CHANGE IF THE NEXT BENCH RUN
;  STILL FAILS
; ======================================================================
;  A fill row is capped separately from a copy row, and today the cap
;  is the same as the register field allows - so this refuses nothing
;  and costs one comparison.
;
;  IT EXISTS BECAUSE OF WHAT A SHORT FILL LOOKS LIKE. The 2026-08-26
;  failure wrote 32 correct bytes and then garbage, on a framebuffer
;  that would be a partially drawn frame, and a partially drawn frame
;  reads as a rendering bug three layers up. The diagnosis says the
;  cause was the pattern size and the pattern is now 256 bytes; if the
;  bracketing cases in the gate's group A2 come back and the limit is
;  STILL there at some row length, then it is a property of the part
;  and not of this file, and the honest response is a refusal with a
;  name rather than a comment nobody reads.
;
;  IN THAT CASE: set #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES to the largest row length
;  that group A2 reported as passing, and write the bench date and the
;  measured boundary beside it. Nothing else has to change - the check
;  is already wired into DmaFillPattern32() and #DMA_ERR_ROWLIMIT
;  already has its sentence in DmaErrorText().
#DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES = 65535   ; = #DMA_MAX_ROW_BYTES: no restriction


; ======================================================================
;  THE BURST CEILING. THIS ONE IS NOT A PRECAUTION - IT IS MEASURED,
;  AND WHAT IT PREVENTS IS A BOARD THAT STOPS ANSWERING.
; ======================================================================
;  Section 4.3 warns about it in one sentence (:5238-5240): "if a read
;  burst is selected that is too large, the AXI read bus will be
;  stalled until the DMA has written out the data. This may lead to
;  inefficient system operation, and possibly AXI lock up if it causes
;  a circular dependency."
;
;  ON 2026-08-26 THAT SENTENCE CAME TRUE ON THIS BOARD. pi4DmaGate.pi4
;  group F swept the burst length above 8 and the board stopped dead:
;  nothing more on the wire, and the next thing seen was Anvil's cold
;  banner after the deadman reset it at 15 s. Not a wrong picture, not
;  a timeout, not an error flag - a hang. DmaWait()'s two-second
;  deadline never fired, because the poll is itself an access over the
;  fabric that had locked.
;
;  WHICH burst length did it is not yet known - group F printed only
;  failures, so the rung that hung left no trace. That is fixed in the
;  gate, and until a run comes back with the number:
;
;    * 8 IS PROVEN. Every case in groups A, A2, B, C, D and E ran at 8
;      on 2026-08-26 and all of them agreed with the CPU, including
;      rows of 4096 bytes.
;    * 9 TO 15 ARE UNPROVEN AND ONE OF THEM LOCKS THE BUS.
;
;  So the cap is 8, and DmaSetBurst() REFUSES above it. This is not the
;  same kind of guard as #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES, which is inert until
;  somebody measures a limit; this one is live now, because the failure
;  it prevents is not a bad frame, it is hands on the board.
;
;  WHEN THE GATE REPORTS THE HANGING RUNG: set this to one below it and
;  write the bench date beside it. If the hang turns out to be at 9,
;  this stays at 8 and the ladder was only ever going to confirm it.
#DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE = 8


; ======================================================================
;  STATE
; ======================================================================
Global dma_scratch.i  = 0      ; base of the caller's scratch block
Global dma_scratchN.i = 0      ; its length in bytes
Global dma_bounds.i   = 0      ; base of the one writable window
Global dma_boundsN.i  = 0      ; its length in bytes
Global dma_haveBounds.i = 0    ; 1 once DmaSetBounds() has been obeyed
Global dma_chan.i     = 0      ; the channel this file drives
Global dma_ready.i    = 0      ; 1 once DmaInit() has succeeded
Global dma_err.i      = 0      ; the last error, one of #DMA_ERR_*
; MEASURED ON SILICON, 2026-08-26, by pi4DmaGate.pi4 on a BCM2711: the
; probe's source pointer advanced EIGHT bytes for a one-row request, so
; the legacy engine follows the same rule the DMA4 table states and the
; legacy table omits - YLENGTH IS ROWS MINUS ONE.
;
; That is why this starts at 1 rather than at -1 and why DmaInit() does
; not probe by default any more: the answer is known for this part, and
; a bring-up should not pay for a measurement whose result is already
; written down. DmaSetYLengthBias(-1) puts it back to "unknown", which
; makes DmaInit() measure it again - both diagnostics do exactly that,
; so every bench run still re-derives the number and prints it, and a
; different part would be caught the first time it ran.
#DMA_YLENGTH_BIAS_BCM2711 = 1
Global dma_ybias.i    = 1      ; 0 if YLENGTH is a count, 1 if it is N-1.
                               ; -1 means "unknown, measure it".
Global dma_burst.i    = 8      ; TI.BURST_LENGTH. See DmaSetBurst().
Global dma_flushaddr.i = 0     ; the one line DmaCacheLine() maintains
; The bounds DmaCacheLines()'s walk reads. They are FILE-SCOPE and not the
; procedure's own locals, and that changed on 2026-09-11 when the compiler
; stopped giving automatic storage a fixed address: a local now lives in the
; invocation's frame, so inline assembly naming one as an absolute symbol is
; refused, and rightly - the name would have meant a different place on every
; call. The walk still has to see the bounds from assembly, so the bounds
; move to the layer that can honestly own an address, beside dma_flushaddr,
; which this file has always carried for exactly the same reason.
; THEY ARE THIS FILE'S SCRATCH AND HAVE THE SAME ONE-CALLER-AT-A-TIME
; CONTRACT dma_flushaddr has: the pair is written and consumed inside one
; call and means nothing between calls.
Global dma_walklo.i = 0        ; first line of the walk, rounded down
Global dma_walkhi.i = 0        ; exclusive end of the walk
Global dma_lastTicks.i = 0     ; CNTPCT_EL0 elapsed over the last wait
Global dma_lastCs.i   = 0      ; CS as it read at the end of the last wait
Global dma_lastDebug.i = 0     ; DEBUG likewise


; ======================================================================
;  THE ARCHITECTURAL COUNTER
;
;  Read directly, for mailbox.pi4's reason (mailbox.pi4:925-933):
;  CNTPCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0 need no setup and no trap at the exception
;  levels a loader hands a program, which makes them the cheapest
;  honest clock a library with no dependencies can have. armstub8.S
;  sets CNTFRQ_EL0 from OSC_FREQ, 54000000 on BCM2711
;  (Datasheets/pi4/armstub8.S:54 and :110-112).
;
;  A program that arrived some other way may find CNTFRQ_EL0 zero. That
;  is handled rather than divided by: DmaWait() falls back to a bounded
;  spin count, so an engine that never answers is a refusal with a code
;  and not a hang with no message.
;
;  THE NAMES ARE DmaTicks() AND DmaCounterHz() ON PURPOSE. timer.pi4
;  owns Ticks() and TickHz() and reads the same two registers; a
;  program that includes both files must not find two definitions of
;  one name.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i DmaTicks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaCounterHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  CACHE MAINTENANCE
;
;  The shape is genet.pi4:1300-1345's and mailbox.pi4:948-970's, and
;  the operation is the same one for the same reason: the other party
;  is a bus master beyond the point of coherency, so a line must be
;  pushed OUT and then DROPPED. That is "dc civac", not "dc cvac" and
;  not "dc cvau".
;
;    dc cvac   cleans only. Right when the reader is a device and the
;              CPU will not read the bytes back - display.pi4's case.
;    dc civac  cleans AND invalidates. Right here, because this file
;              maintains buffers that travel in BOTH directions: the
;              control block and the pattern go out to the engine, the
;              calibration result comes back from it, and a caller's
;              destination rectangle comes back from it too.
;    dc ivac   would be the literal "invalidate", and it is wrong twice
;              over: it is an EL1-or-above operation, and it DISCARDS a
;              dirty line, which would lose a control block the CPU had
;              just written. xhci.pi4:295-303 works through the same
;              choice at length.
;
;  ENCODING, from the family table at anvil.pi4:1428-1429:
;    dc civac, x9   op1=3 CRn=7 CRm=14 op2=1 Rt=9  -> $D50B7E29
;
;  "ldr x9", NOT "ldr w9". dma_flushaddr is a .i and a .i is EIGHT
;  bytes on this target. ldr w9 assembles cleanly, uses the right
;  register, and silently drops the top half of the address. Every
;  file in this tree that hand-writes this sequence says so, because it
;  is the shape of bug the widening creates.
;
;  This library does not infer whether maintenance can be skipped from
;  the caller's current MMU state. Anvil can enable cached execution,
;  and a payload can use the library under its own mappings. Preserve
;  the explicit maintenance contract in either case.
; ======================================================================
Procedure DmaCacheLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_dma_flushaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_dma_flushaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc civac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaCacheRange - clean and invalidate every line the range touches,
; then one barrier for the whole range.
;
; PUBLIC, and the caller needs it. This file maintains its own control
; block and its own pattern and nothing else; a caller who has just
; written a source rectangle with the CPU must clean it before asking
; for a copy, and a caller who is about to READ pixels the engine wrote
; must invalidate them first. Both are this one call.
;
; The start is rounded DOWN and the end UP, which is what U-Boot's
; cache_v8.c does and for the same reason: a partial line still has to
; be maintained. Rounding out can only touch a neighbouring line, and
; clean-and-invalidate of a neighbour costs a refill and loses nothing,
; because civac writes back dirty data before dropping the line.
;
; ROUNDED DOWN BY DIVIDE-AND-MULTIPLY, NOT BY MASKING. A mask written
; as "addr & $FFFFFFC0" is a 32-bit literal and would clear the top
; half of a 64-bit address along with the low six bits. The addresses
; here are physical and therefore positive, so the divide is exact.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaCacheLines - the same walk WITHOUT the barrier.
;
; Internal, and split out on 2026-08-27 when DmaCacheRect below needed to
; maintain many rows and pay for ONE barrier rather than one per row.
;
; NOTHING OUTSIDE THIS FILE MAY CALL IT. A maintenance sequence with no
; barrier after it is not finished: the dc operations are not guaranteed
; complete, so a transfer started immediately afterwards can race them.
; That is the whole reason DmaCacheRange keeps the barrier inside itself
; rather than making every caller remember - and it is why this one is
; named for the operation rather than for the range.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DmaCacheLines(addr.i, len.i)
  Protected a.i
  Protected e.i
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  a = (addr / #DMA_CACHE_LINE) * #DMA_CACHE_LINE
  e = addr + len
  ; Hand the bounds to the walk through this file's own storage. The walk
  ; reads them from assembly and an automatic local has no address assembly
  ; can name - it lives in the frame of one invocation - so the two values
  ; are written to dma_walklo/dma_walkhi here, one store each, before the
  ; loop starts.
  dma_walklo = a
  dma_walkhi = e
  ; Load the full-width bounds once. The hot loop has no procedure call or
  ; shared scratch store per cache line; maintenance still uses CIVAC and
  ; the public range/rectangle caller still owns its final barrier. These
  ; are positive physical addresses with a non-overflowing exclusive end,
  ; exactly the existing range contract. BCM2711's D-cache line is 64 bytes.
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_dma_walklo
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_dma_walklo
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    adrp x10, global_dma_walkhi
    add  x10, x10, #:lo12:global_dma_walkhi
    ldr  x10, [x10]
    cmp  x9, x10
    b.ge dmaCacheWalkDone
  dmaCacheWalkNext:
    dc   civac, x9
    add  x9, x9, #64
    cmp  x9, x10
    b.lt dmaCacheWalkNext
  dmaCacheWalkDone:
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ONE BEHAVIOUR CHANGED IN THE 2026-08-27 SPLIT AND IT IS NAMED HERE
; RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE NOTICED. The zero-length early return now lives
; in DmaCacheLines, so DmaCacheRange(addr, 0) issues a barrier where
; before it returned having issued nothing. That is strictly the safer
; direction - a dsb with no maintenance in front of it costs cycles and
; can never be wrong - and it is left that way rather than testing the
; length twice. Nothing in this tree calls it with zero.
Procedure DmaCacheRange(addr.i, len.i)
  DmaCacheLines(addr, len)
  a64_barrier()                  ; dsb sy + isb, from the intrinsics
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaCacheRect - maintain a RECTANGLE: rows runs of rowBytes, pitch
; apart, with ONE barrier for the whole thing.
;
; PUBLIC, and it is the call a compositor actually needs. A rectangle is
; not contiguous in memory, so DmaCacheRange over "the first byte to the
; last byte" would also maintain everything to the left and right of the
; rectangle on every row - correct, and on a narrow column many times
; the work. display.pi4:2249-2291 makes the same argument from the CPU
; side and walks its rows the same way.
;
; ONE BARRIER, NOT ONE PER ROW, and that is the reason this exists at all
; rather than the caller looping over DmaCacheRange. A 1080-row surface
; would otherwise pay 1080 "dsb sy; isb" pairs for one region, and a dsb
; on this part waits for every outstanding memory operation in the
; machine, not just the ones this loop issued.
;
; THE CONTIGUOUS CASE IS COLLAPSED, and the condition is exact: when the
; pitch EQUALS the row there is no gap between one row and the next, so
; the whole rectangle is one run and walking it as rows would re-derive
; the same addresses in a slower loop. This is the same special case
; display.pi4's DspFlushSpan carves out and it carries the same warning:
; it is correct ONLY when the region really is contiguous, which for a
; surface means whole rows AND pitch = width * bpp.
;
; IT DOES NOT CHECK REACHABILITY and it must not. Cache maintenance is a
; CPU operation on CPU addresses; the 1 GiB cap is a property of the
; ENGINE's address bus and has nothing to say about which lines the CPU
; may clean. A caller staging through a buffer above the cap still has to
; maintain it before copying it down.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DmaCacheRect(addr.i, pitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i)
  Protected r.i
  If rowBytes <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If rows <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  If pitch = rowBytes
    DmaCacheLines(addr, rowBytes * rows)
  Else
    r = 0
    While r < rows
      DmaCacheLines(addr + (r * pitch), rowBytes)
      r = r + 1
    Wend
  EndIf

  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  REGISTER ACCESS
;
;  Peripheral registers are 32 bits and every access here says so.
;  PeekN is the unsigned 32-bit read - the .n type exists precisely so
;  a register with bit 31 set does not arrive as a negative 64-bit
;  value - and PokeL is the 32-bit write. A bare Peek or Poke would be
;  EIGHT bytes on this target and would take the neighbouring register
;  with it; genet.pi4:1247-1279 and gpio.pi4:64-66 both record that
;  trap.
;
;  There is no offset validation here of the kind genet_Peek() does,
;  and the reason is that no offset in this file comes from a caller.
;  Every one is a named constant from the table above. The values that
;  DO come from a caller - addresses, widths, strides - are checked
;  where they arrive, which is the entry points.
; ======================================================================

; The base of the channel currently selected.
Procedure.i DmaChanBase()
  ProcedureReturn #DMA_BASE + (dma_chan * #DMA_CHAN_STEP)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaRegRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(DmaChanBase() + off)
EndProcedure

Procedure DmaRegWrite(off.i, value.i)
  PokeL(DmaChanBase() + off, value & $FFFFFFFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaGlobalRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#DMA_BASE + off)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  TIMEOUTS
;
;  Two deadlines, both checked, for mailbox.pi4's reason
;  (mailbox.pi4:839-843): the tick deadline is the real one, and the
;  spin limit is the backstop for a board where CNTFRQ_EL0 reads zero
;  because nothing set it. An unbounded spin on a device that never
;  answers is a hang with no message, which is the one outcome a
;  library in this tree refuses to have.
;
;  TWO SECONDS is far outside any legitimate transfer on this part: the
;  CPU itself clears the whole 8.29 MB surface in 266 ms, and the whole
;  reason this file exists is to be several times faster than that. A
;  transfer still running after two seconds is not slow, it is stuck.
; ======================================================================
#DMA_TIMEOUT_US  = 2000000
#DMA_SPIN_LIMIT  = 200000000

; How long to spin after writing CS.RESET before believing the channel
; is quiescent. RESET "cannot be read, and will self clear"
; (:3035-3040), so there is nothing to poll; the reset is a handful of
; cycles of internal state and this is generous by orders of magnitude.
#DMA_RESET_SPINS = 10000


; ======================================================================
;  SIMPLE ACCESSORS
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i DmaError()
  ProcedureReturn dma_err
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaReady()
  ProcedureReturn dma_ready
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaChannel()
  ProcedureReturn dma_chan
EndProcedure

; The one gigabyte the 30-bit engines can reach. See HAZARD 1.
Procedure.i DmaLimit()
  ProcedureReturn #DMA_LIMIT
EndProcedure

; 0 if YLENGTH turned out to be a plain row count, 1 if it turned out
; to be rows minus one, -1 if DmaInit() has not measured it yet. The
; vendor PDF does not say which; see the header section on it.
Procedure.i DmaYLengthBias()
  ProcedureReturn dma_ybias
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaMaxRowBytes()
  ProcedureReturn #DMA_MAX_ROW_BYTES
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaMaxRows()
  ProcedureReturn #DMA_MAX_ROWS
EndProcedure

; Raw register values as they read at the end of the last DmaWait().
; For a diagnostic to print, and for nothing else - a caller that
; decides what to do next by reading CS itself has reached around this
; file rather than through it.
Procedure.i DmaLastCs()
  ProcedureReturn dma_lastCs
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaLastDebug()
  ProcedureReturn dma_lastDebug
EndProcedure

; CNTPCT_EL0 ticks the last transfer spent between ACTIVE going up and
; ACTIVE coming down. Divide by DmaCounterHz() for seconds. This is the
; number the comparison against the CPU's 266 ms is made from, and it
; is measured INSIDE this file so that the caller's own timing does not
; have to include the control-block setup - though it should, and the
; diagnostic times both.
Procedure.i DmaLastTicks()
  ProcedureReturn dma_lastTicks
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  REACHABILITY - the check that belongs to this file
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaAddrOk - may THIS engine read or write these bytes?
;
; This is not PcieDmaOk(). That one answers a different question about
; a different bus master and its cap is $C0000000; this one is
; $40000000 and it is the tighter of the two by three quarters of the
; address space. HAZARD 1 in the header sets out why there are two.
;
; A zero or negative length is refused rather than waved through, on
; pcie.pi4:675's reasoning: a zero-length transfer is a caller that
; computed a size wrongly, and answering "yes, fine" to it hides the
; arithmetic error one call earlier.
;
; The end is checked as well as the start, so a buffer that BEGINS
; below the cap and RUNS OVER it is refused. That case is not
; hypothetical here - a full-surface framebuffer is megabytes long.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaAddrOk(addr.i, len.i)
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If addr <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If addr >= #DMA_LIMIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (addr + len) > #DMA_LIMIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaBusAddr - the ARM physical address, as the engine must be told it.
;
; The engines address DRAM through the VideoCore's UNCACHED alias at
; $C0000000, which is the "1G uncached range C000_0000->ffff_ffff" the
; ENABLE register's PAGE field talks about (:4994-5002) and the bus
; side of bcm2711.dtsi:45's dma-ranges. The low 30 bits are the offset
; into DRAM; the top two select the VPU's cache behaviour, and uncached
; is the one a bus master wants - it is the whole point of doing the
; cache maintenance on the ARM side instead.
;
; This is the mirror of display.pi4:2053's "arm = bus & $3FFFFFFF",
; running the other way.
;
; It does NOT check reachability. DmaAddrOk() does that, and every
; entry point calls it first; keeping the two apart means a diagnostic
; can print the bus address of an unreachable buffer and see what the
; engine would have been told.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaBusAddr(physAddr.i)
  ProcedureReturn #DMA_BUS_ALIAS | (physAddr & #DMA_BUS_MASK)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SETUP
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSetScratch - hand this file the small block it works in.
;
; 256-BYTE ALIGNED, 512 BYTES LONG, and both numbers grew on
; 2026-08-26 when the 32-byte fill pattern turned out to be smaller
; than one read burst - the long section above the constants is the
; whole story. A caller that was passing 128 bytes will now be refused
; with #DMA_ERR_SMALL, which is the correct outcome: silently working
; with a block too small for the pattern is exactly the bug that
; reached silicon.
;
; Below the 1 GiB cap because the engine reads the control block and
; the pattern out of it.
;
; Calling this again clears the ready flag. The scratch block belongs
; to the caller and this file has no way to know it has not been
; handed somewhere else in between, so a library that stayed "ready"
; while pointing at memory the caller may have reused would be
; asserting something it cannot know.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSetScratch(base.i, bytes.i)
  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If base <= 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOSCRATCH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (base & (#DMA_SCRATCH_ALIGN - 1)) <> 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ALIGN
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If bytes < #DMA_SCRATCH_BYTES
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_SMALL
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaAddrOk(base, bytes) = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_REACH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  dma_scratch  = base
  dma_scratchN = bytes
  dma_ready    = 0
  ; dma_ybias is deliberately NOT reset here. Which way YLENGTH counts
  ; is a property of the PART, not of the memory the control block
  ; happens to live in, and throwing the answer away because somebody
  ; moved the scratch block would make a caller pay for a measurement
  ; twice for no reason.
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSetBounds - declare the ONE window this file may write into.
;
; MANDATORY. HAZARD 3 in the header says why at length; the short form
; is that this engine writes physical addresses with nothing in the
; path to stop it, and the failure mode of a wrong destination is a
; board that stops answering rather than a picture that looks wrong.
;
; Normally the framebuffer:
;
;     DmaSetBounds(DisplayBase(), DisplaySize())
;
; A caller that legitimately blits into two places - a shadow buffer
; and the surface - calls this again between the two. It does not
; clear the ready flag, because nothing about the channel or the
; calibration depends on where the pixels are going.
;
; The window must itself be reachable, because a destination inside an
; unreachable window is unreachable. Refusing here rather than at the
; first transfer puts the error next to the mistake.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSetBounds(base.i, bytes.i)
  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If base <= 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If bytes <= 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaAddrOk(base, bytes) = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_REACH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  dma_bounds     = base
  dma_boundsN    = bytes
  dma_haveBounds = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSetChannel - drive a different channel.
;
; The default is 0 and there is a reason for it: channel 0 is one of
; the two fitted with an external 128-bit 8-word read FIFO, which the
; PDF says is what "enables efficient memory to memory transfers to be
; performed" (:5236-5238). The other one is channel 15 and that belongs
; to the VPU (:2293-2294).
;
; WHAT IS REFUSED, AND WHY EACH:
;
;   1 and 3   Clear in brcm,dma-channel-mask = <0x07f5>
;             (Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:106), which means the
;             firmware has them. Taking one is the kind of fault that
;             shows up as an unrelated peripheral misbehaving.
;   7 to 10   LITE engines. "The Lite engine does not support 2D
;             transfers. The TDMODE, S_STRIDE, D_STRIDE and YLENGTH
;             registers will all be removed. Setting these registers
;             will have no effect." (:5269-5271). Refused with a
;             DIFFERENT code from the others, because the mistake is a
;             different mistake: the channel exists and is available,
;             it simply cannot do the one thing this file does. A
;             silently-ignored TDMODE would produce a LINEAR transfer
;             whose length is {YLENGTH, XLENGTH} - millions of bytes
;             written straight through the surface and out the far
;             side.
;   11 to 14  DMA4. Real hardware, better hardware, completely
;             different control-block format. See the last section of
;             the header.
;   15        The VPU's.
;
; So 0, 2, 4, 5 and 6 are the five this file will accept.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSetChannel(ch.i)
  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If ch < 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ch > 6
    If ch < 11
      dma_err = #DMA_ERR_LITE          ; 7..10, the LITE engines
    Else
      dma_err = #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL       ; 11..14 DMA4, 15 the VPU's
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ch = 1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL         ; the firmware's, per the mask
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ch = 3
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL         ; the firmware's, per the mask
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  dma_chan  = ch
  dma_ready = 0
  ; Nor here, and for the same reason: channels 0, 2, 4, 5 and 6 are
  ; the same engine design on the same die. If a part ever turned up
  ; where they were not, DmaSetYLengthBias(-1) is how to say so.
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSetBurst - TI.BURST_LENGTH, bits 15:12 (:3294-3302).
;
; "The DMA will attempt to transfer data as bursts of this number of
; words. A value of zero will produce a single transfer."
;
; This knob exists because it is the ONE parameter that materially
; changes throughput and the whole deliverable here is a measurement
; against the CPU's 266 ms. A diagnostic that cannot sweep it cannot
; say whether the default was a good default.
;
; THE DEFAULT IS 8 AND THAT IS NOT ARBITRARY. Section 4.3 (:5230-5240)
; says up to 16 beats can be accommodated, that channel 0's external
; FIFO allows "a 128-bit wide read burst of 9", and - the sentence that
; sets the ceiling - "if a read burst is selected that is too large,
; the AXI read bus will be stalled until the DMA has written out the
; data. This may lead to inefficient system operation, and possibly AXI
; lock up if it causes a circular dependency." Eight is inside what the
; FIFO can absorb on channel 0 and inside what a channel without one
; can absorb on a 256-bit internal register.
;
; IT IS CAPPED AT #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE AND THE CAP IS NOT ADVISORY.
; The long note beside that constant has the bench evidence; the short
; form is that a burst length above 8 locked the AXI bus on this board
; on 2026-08-26 and the deadman had to reset it. A value that hangs the
; machine is not a performance choice a caller gets to make, so this
; refuses it with #DMA_ERR_BURST rather than clamping silently - a
; silent clamp would leave a caller believing it had asked for
; something it had not, which is how a performance regression becomes
; unexplainable.
;
; THE INSTRUMENT THAT HAS TO EXCEED THE CAP USES DmaSetBurstProbe(),
; below. Earlier this procedure accepted up to 15 with a comment saying
; "a sweep that cannot reach the bad values cannot find the edge" -
; that reasoning is still right and it now belongs to the one caller it
; was ever true for, instead of to everybody.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSetBurst(n.i)
  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE
  If n < 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If n > #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_BURST
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dma_burst = n
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSetBurstProbe - THE SAME KNOB WITH THE SAFETY OFF. One caller.
;
; This exists for RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DmaGate.pi4's
; group F and for nothing else, because a ladder that cannot climb past
; the cap cannot discover where the cap should be. It accepts the full
; four-bit field, 0 to 15.
;
; IF YOU ARE ABOUT TO CALL THIS FROM ANYTHING THAT DRAWS, DO NOT. The
; failure mode is not a bad rectangle. It is the AXI read bus stalling
; with no way back: no error flag, no timeout - DmaWait()'s own poll is
; an access over the same fabric - and no software recovery. The board
; needs a reset. Anything aimed at a framebuffer should be inside
; DmaSetBurst()'s cap, which is what that cap is for.
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN BEFORE RUNNING ANYTHING THAT CALLS THIS. `deadman 15`
; at the Anvil prompt, then `run`. It is the difference between a
; fifteen-second reset and hands on the board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSetBurstProbe(n.i)
  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE
  If n < 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If n > 15
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dma_burst = n
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The cap, so a diagnostic can derive its sweep ceiling from the
; library rather than hardcoding a number that then drifts away from
; it. pi4DmaProbe.pi4's burst sweep stops here for exactly that reason.
Procedure.i DmaBurstMaxSafe()
  ProcedureReturn #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaBurst()
  ProcedureReturn dma_burst
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE ENGINE
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaBusy - is the channel still moving data?
;
; CS bit 0 ACTIVE, which "is automatically cleared at the end of the
; complete DMA transfer, i.e. after a NEXTCONBK = 0x0000_0000 has been
; loaded" (:3201-3210). Every control block this file builds has a zero
; NEXTCONBK, so ACTIVE going low is the completion signal.
;
; AND IT MEANS THE PIXELS ARE IN MEMORY, not merely on their way,
; because every start sets CS.WAIT_FOR_OUTSTANDING_WRITES. The header
; section on that bit says why it is the right one and TI.WAIT_RESP is
; not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaBusy()
  If (DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS) & #DMA_CS_ACTIVE) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaAbort - stop a channel that is not going to finish.
;
; Internal, and called only from the timeout path. The sequence is the
; PDF's own, from section 4.4 (:5252-5254): "abort the DMA transfer by
; clearing the NEXTCONBK register and restarting the DMA with the ABORT
; bit set". NEXTCONBK is already zero in every control block here, so
; what is left is ABORT and then RESET.
;
; RESET as well as ABORT, because ABORT only drops the CURRENT control
; block - "The DMA will load the next CB and attempt to continue"
; (:3042-3048) - and a channel that has gone wrong badly enough to miss
; a two-second deadline should not be left holding any state at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DmaAbort()
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, #DMA_CS_ABORT)
  a64_barrier()
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, #DMA_CS_RESET)
  a64_barrier()
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_DEBUG, #DMA_DEBUG_ERROR_MASK)
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, #DMA_CS_END | #DMA_CS_INT)
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaChannelReset - put the channel into a known state.
;
; Writing 1 to CS bit 31 resets the channel. The bit "cannot be read,
; and will self clear" (:3035-3040), so there is nothing to poll for
; the reset itself; what CAN be polled is the consequence, which is
; ACTIVE going low, and that is what this loop waits for.
;
; The DEBUG write clears the three sticky error latches. They are
; write-1-to-clear and they survive a reset, so a stale READ_ERROR from
; a previous program would otherwise be read as ours the first time
; anything checked CS.ERROR - the error bits "are logically OR-ed
; together and presented as a general ERROR bit in the CS register"
; (:5257-5258).
;
; The final CS write clears END and INT, which are also W1C. Writing
; only those two bits is deliberate: a lazy "write zero to CS" would be
; harmless, but a lazy "write $FFFFFFFF to clear everything" would set
; RESET and ABORT again, and that habit is how a clear-the-flags line
; turns into an intermittent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaChannelReset()
  Protected i.i
  Protected cs.i

  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, #DMA_CS_RESET)
  a64_barrier()

  i  = 0
  cs = DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS)
  While i < #DMA_RESET_SPINS
    If (cs & #DMA_CS_ACTIVE) = 0
      i = #DMA_RESET_SPINS
    Else
      i  = i + 1
      cs = DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS)
    EndIf
  Wend

  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_DEBUG, #DMA_DEBUG_ERROR_MASK)
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, #DMA_CS_END | #DMA_CS_INT)
  a64_barrier()

  If (DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS) & #DMA_CS_ACTIVE) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaWait - block until the channel finishes, or refuse.
;
; TWO DEADLINES, both checked every pass, exactly as mailbox.pi4 does
; (mailbox.pi4:1000-1030). The tick deadline is the real one. The spin
; count is the backstop for a board where CNTFRQ_EL0 reads zero because
; nothing set it, and it is what turns "the engine never answered" from
; a silent hang into a code and a message.
;
; ON TIMEOUT THE CHANNEL IS ABORTED. That is not tidiness. A transfer
; that has not finished in two seconds is a bus master still writing
; somewhere, and returning to the caller while it does that is how a
; diagnostic's next screenful gets overwritten by the previous one.
;
; THE POLL IS A TIGHT LOOP ON A DEVICE REGISTER and that is a real cost
; the measurement should know about: every read is an uncached round
; trip to the peripheral bus and it competes for the same fabric the
; engine is using. It is not enough to change the answer at these
; sizes - a full-surface fill is milliseconds and a CS read is
; nanoseconds - but on a small rectangle the poll can dominate, which
; is one more reason the asynchronous door is left open.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaWait(timeoutUs.i)
  Protected hz.i
  Protected t0.i
  Protected limit.i
  Protected spins.i
  Protected cs.i
  Protected dbg.i
  Protected waiting.i
  Protected rc.i

  hz = DmaCounterHz()
  t0 = DmaTicks()

  limit = 0
  If hz > 0
    limit = (hz / 1000000) * timeoutUs
  EndIf

  rc      = 1
  waiting = 1
  spins   = 0
  cs      = DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS)

  While waiting = 1
    cs = DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS)
    If (cs & #DMA_CS_ACTIVE) = 0
      waiting = 0
    Else
      spins = spins + 1
      If spins > #DMA_SPIN_LIMIT
        waiting = 0
        rc      = 0
        dma_err = #DMA_ERR_TIMEOUT
      Else
        If limit > 0
          If (DmaTicks() - t0) > limit
            waiting = 0
            rc      = 0
            dma_err = #DMA_ERR_TIMEOUT
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Wend

  dma_lastTicks = DmaTicks() - t0
  dma_lastCs    = cs
  dbg           = DmaRegRead(#DMA_DEBUG)
  dma_lastDebug = dbg

  If rc = 0
    DmaAbort()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; CS.ERROR is the OR of the three DEBUG latches (:5257-5258). Both are
  ; checked, and separately, because a DEBUG latch left over from an
  ; earlier transfer that this file did not clear would show in DEBUG
  ; and not in CS - and the reverse ordering of that discovery is worth
  ; making impossible.
  If (cs & #DMA_CS_ERROR) <> 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_HW
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (dbg & #DMA_DEBUG_ERROR_MASK) <> 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_HW
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSubmit - write the control block, start the channel, wait.
;
; Internal. Every public operation funnels through here, which is the
; point: there is exactly ONE place in this file that writes CONBLK_AD
; and sets ACTIVE, so there is exactly one place where the cache
; discipline and the start ordering can be wrong.
;
; THE CONTROL BLOCK IS EIGHT WORDS AND THE LAST TWO ARE NOT OPTIONAL.
; Table 34 (:2351-2380) lists words 6 and 7 as "Reserved - set to
; zero." They are written every time rather than once at init, because
; the scratch block belongs to the caller and this file has no way to
; know it has not been used for something else in between.
;
; THE ORDER OF THE LAST FOUR STEPS IS THE PDF'S (:2442-2448) AND EACH
; ONE MATTERS:
;
;   1. clear the sticky flags, so END means THIS transfer's end
;   2. clean the control block out of the D-cache, so the engine reads
;      what the CPU wrote and not what DRAM last saw. HAZARD 2.
;   3. write CONBLK_AD with the BUS address of the block
;   4. barrier, then set ACTIVE
;
; Steps 2 and 3 cannot be swapped. The engine begins fetching the block
; the moment ACTIVE goes up, and a clean that arrived after that is a
; race that will work on a small transfer and fail on a busy one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSubmit(ti.i, srcBus.i, dstBus.i, lenWord.i, strideWord.i)
  Protected cb.i
  Protected cs.i

  cb = dma_scratch + #DMA_SCRATCH_CB

  PokeL(cb +  0, ti)
  PokeL(cb +  4, srcBus)
  PokeL(cb +  8, dstBus)
  PokeL(cb + 12, lenWord)
  PokeL(cb + 16, strideWord)
  PokeL(cb + 20, 0)              ; NEXTCONBK. One block, no chain.
  PokeL(cb + 24, 0)              ; reserved - "set to zero", :2377
  PokeL(cb + 28, 0)              ; reserved - "set to zero", :2377

  ; Clean the control block AND the fill pattern in one range. They are
  ; 64 contiguous bytes at the base of the scratch block, so this is one
  ; or two cache lines and one barrier, and the barrier is the part that
  ; matters: it is what orders the writes above ahead of the ACTIVE
  ; below.
  DmaCacheRange(dma_scratch, #DMA_SCRATCH_PATTERN + #DMA_PATTERN_BYTES)
  ; That is the whole 512 bytes - control block, the reserved gap, and
  ; all 256 bytes of the pattern. Cleaning the gap costs three cache
  ; lines nobody needed and removes a question: after 2026-08-26 the
  ; rule in this file is that everything the engine can possibly read
  ; out of the scratch block is cleaned, not everything this file
  ; believes it will read.

  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_DEBUG, #DMA_DEBUG_ERROR_MASK)
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, #DMA_CS_END | #DMA_CS_INT)
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CONBLK_AD, DmaBusAddr(cb))
  a64_barrier()

  cs = #DMA_CS_ACTIVE
  cs = cs | #DMA_CS_WAIT_WRITES
  cs = cs | (#DMA_DEFAULT_PRIORITY << #DMA_PRIORITY_SHIFT)
  cs = cs | (#DMA_DEFAULT_PRIORITY << #DMA_PANIC_PRIORITY_SHIFT)
  DmaRegWrite(#DMA_CS, cs)

  ProcedureReturn DmaWait(#DMA_TIMEOUT_US)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE YLENGTH CALIBRATION
; ======================================================================
; The question, restated because it is the one unresolved thing in the
; vendor documentation for this engine: in 2D mode, is TXFR_LEN's
; YLENGTH field the NUMBER of rows, or the number of rows MINUS ONE?
;
;   Legacy, Table 43, :3477-3480 - "indicating how many xlength
;   transfers are performed", and nothing else.
;   DMA4, Table 60, :4773-4779 - the same sentence, followed by "In 2D
;   mode a value of 0 will result in a single XLENGTH transfer and a
;   value of 1 will result in 2 XLENGTH transfers."
;
; The DMA4 table is explicit and the legacy table is silent, so the
; legacy engine is either convention and the document does not say.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS DOES NOT FILL A BUFFER AND COUNT WHAT CHANGED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; That was the first design and it was thrown away, because of what it
; does when the answer is neither of the two expected ones.
;
; A calibration that WRITES has to ask for at least one row, and asking
; for one row means putting a non-zero value in YLENGTH. If TDMODE were
; ignored for any reason, TXFR_LEN reverts to a single linear transfer
; of {YLENGTH, XLENGTH} bytes - and YLENGTH occupies bits 29:16, so the
; smallest non-zero value there is SIXTY-FIVE THOUSAND FIVE HUNDRED AND
; THIRTY-SIX BYTES. A probe meant to move sixteen bytes would move 64
; KiB, through the scratch block and into whatever the caller put after
; it. There is no value of XLENGTH that bounds that, because the
; overflow is in the other field.
;
; TI.DEST_IGNORE removes the problem completely: "1 = Do not perform
; destination writes" (:3336-3342). The probe reads and writes nothing,
; so the worst case of every wrong answer is a few kilobytes of
; pointless READS out of the scratch block - which cost nothing and
; corrupt nothing.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW IT COUNTS ROWS WITH NOTHING WRITTEN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; By asking the engine. SOURCE_AD is documented as "Source address for
; the DMA operation. Updated by the DMA engine as the transfer
; progresses." (:3520-3527) - it is a read-only mirror of the CB word
; that the hardware advances, and it is still there to be read when the
; channel stops.
;
; So: one row of four bytes, YLENGTH = 1, SRC_INC set, SRC_WIDTH clear
; (32-bit reads, so the address moves in fours), BURST_LENGTH zero (so
; the engine cannot read ahead past what was asked for), S_STRIDE zero.
; Afterwards SOURCE_AD has advanced by four bytes per row performed:
;
;     advance 4   -> YLENGTH is a plain count. bias = 0.
;     advance 8   -> YLENGTH is rows minus one. bias = 1.
;     advance 65540 -> TDMODE was ignored and the transfer went linear.
;                      That should be impossible on channels 0/2/4/5/6
;                      and DmaInit() has already checked DEBUG.LITE, so
;                      it is reported as a calibration failure rather
;                      than quietly handled.
;     anything else -> the assumption that SOURCE_AD reads back is
;                      wrong on this part. Also a calibration failure,
;                      and DmaSetYLengthBias() is the way past it.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaSetYLengthBias - declare the answer instead of measuring it.
;
; For two cases. The first is the ordinary one: once the probe has run
; on silicon and the answer is written down, a program can state it and
; skip the probe. The second is the escape hatch: if SOURCE_AD does not
; read back the way the PDF says it does, the probe cannot work and
; this is how a bench operator supplies the answer by hand after
; observing which way a test rectangle came out.
;
; Call it BEFORE DmaInit(), which will then leave it alone.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaSetYLengthBias(b.i)
  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE
  If b < -1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If b > 1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  dma_ybias = b
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaCalibrate - run the probe. Returns 1 and sets dma_ybias, or 0.
;
; Public, so a diagnostic can re-run it and print what it found without
; going through DmaInit() again. It needs the scratch block and a
; channel and nothing else; in particular it does NOT need bounds,
; because it does not write.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaCalibrate()
  Protected ti.i
  Protected srcBus.i
  Protected lenWord.i
  Protected endBus.i
  Protected advance.i

  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If dma_scratch = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOSCRATCH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  srcBus = DmaBusAddr(dma_scratch + #DMA_SCRATCH_PATTERN)

  ; TDMODE, because that is what is being measured. SRC_INC so the
  ; address moves. DEST_IGNORE so nothing is written. No SRC_WIDTH, so
  ; the step is four bytes and not thirty-two. No burst, so the engine
  ; does not read further than it was asked to. No DEST_INC and no
  ; DEST_WIDTH, because there are no writes for them to describe.
  ti = 0
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_TDMODE
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_SRC_INC
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_IGNORE

  lenWord = ((#DMA_CALIB_YLEN & $3FFF) << 16) | (#DMA_CALIB_XLEN & $FFFF)

  ; Destination is the pattern area too. It is ignored, but a control
  ; block with a zero destination is a control block that would write
  ; to bus $C0000000 - physical zero, the firmware spin table - if
  ; DEST_IGNORE ever failed to take. Pointing it somewhere harmless
  ; costs nothing and removes the worst outcome from the table.
  If DmaSubmit(ti, srcBus, srcBus, lenWord, 0) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  endBus  = DmaRegRead(#DMA_SOURCE_AD)
  advance = endBus - srcBus

  If advance = #DMA_CALIB_XLEN
    dma_ybias = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If advance = (#DMA_CALIB_XLEN * 2)
    dma_ybias = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  dma_ybias = -1
  dma_err   = #DMA_ERR_CALIB
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; What the probe last saw the source pointer advance by. For a
; diagnostic to print when DmaCalibrate() refuses - the number is the
; whole diagnosis, and 65540 in particular says "TDMODE was ignored".
Procedure.i DmaCalibrateAdvance()
  ProcedureReturn DmaRegRead(#DMA_SOURCE_AD) - DmaBusAddr(dma_scratch + #DMA_SCRATCH_PATTERN)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  BRING-UP
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaInit - claim the channel, check the world, measure YLENGTH.
;
; Returns 1 on success. Returns 0 and leaves DmaError() set otherwise,
; and DmaErrorText() puts every one of those codes into a sentence.
;
; WHAT IT CHECKS, IN THIS ORDER, AND WHY EACH CHECK EARNS ITS PLACE:
;
;   1. THE SCRATCH BLOCK EXISTS. Without it there is nowhere to build a
;      control block and nothing can happen.
;
;   2. THE BOUNDS HAVE BEEN DECLARED. Not needed by anything in
;      DmaInit() itself - the calibration writes nothing - and required
;      here anyway, so that a program cannot reach the state of being
;      "ready" while still one forgotten call away from a transfer that
;      would be refused. HAZARD 3.
;
;   3. THE ENABLE REGISTER'S **PAGE** FIELD IS ZERO. This is the check
;      nobody thinks of and it invalidates every address in this file
;      if it fails. Bits 27:24 of ENABLE "set the 1G SDRAM ram page
;      that the 30-bit DMA engines (DMA0-6) will access when addressing
;      the 1G uncached range C000_0000->ffff_ffff... setting this to 1
;      will mean that when the DMA writes to C000_0000 (uncached) the
;      final address in SDRAM will be 4000_0000" (:4994-5002).
;
;      So a non-zero PAGE means every bus address DmaBusAddr() produces
;      lands one or more gigabytes away from where the caller meant. A
;      full-surface fill would go to $40000000 instead of the
;      framebuffer - into the HIGH payload window, silently, with a
;      black screen as the only symptom. The reset value is 0 and this
;      file never writes the field; if it is not 0, something else on
;      this board moved it and this file must not proceed.
;
;   4. THE CHANNEL'S ENABLE BIT IS SET. Bits 14:0 of the same register,
;      all reset to 1 (:5030-5100). A clear bit means somebody
;      deliberately powered the channel down. This file REFUSES rather
;      than sets it, because the same register's description warns
;      "Disabling whilst the DMA is operating will be fatal" - a
;      register that dangerous is not one to write speculatively, and a
;      cleared bit is information about the system, not an obstacle.
;
;   5. THE CHANNEL IS NOT ALREADY ACTIVE. If it is, somebody else has
;      it: the firmware on a channel the device tree said was free, or
;      a previous run of this program that left a transfer in flight.
;      Resetting a channel out from under a live transfer is how a
;      graphics experiment corrupts an unrelated driver.
;
;   6. THE CHANNEL RESETS CLEANLY, which also clears the three sticky
;      DEBUG error latches so a stale one from a previous program is
;      not read as ours.
;
;   7. DEBUG.LITE IS CLEAR. Bit 28, read-only, "Set if the DMA is a
;      reduced performance LITE engine." The channel whitelist in
;      DmaSetChannel() already excludes 7-10 on the strength of the
;      PDF; this asks the silicon the same question and is the check
;      that would catch a part where the classification differs. It
;      matters more than it looks: a LITE engine silently ignores
;      TDMODE, and a silently-ignored TDMODE turns a 1920x1080 fill
;      into a linear transfer of {YLENGTH, XLENGTH} = seventy million
;      bytes.
;
;   8. THE YLENGTH CONVENTION. On a BCM2711 it is ALREADY KNOWN and
;      nothing is measured: the bias starts at
;      #DMA_YLENGTH_BIAS_BCM2711 = 1, measured on silicon on
;      2026-08-26, and DmaInit() takes it as given. The probe runs only
;      if a caller has said DmaSetYLengthBias(-1) - "forget it, measure
;      it again" - which is what both diagnostics do, so every bench
;      run still re-derives the number and prints it and a different
;      part would be caught the first time it ran.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaInit()
  Protected en.i
  Protected page.i
  Protected dbg.i
  Protected cs.i

  dma_err   = #DMA_ERR_NONE
  dma_ready = 0

  If dma_scratch = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOSCRATCH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If dma_haveBounds = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOBOUNDS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  en   = DmaGlobalRead(#DMA_ENABLE)
  page = (en >> 24) & $F
  If page <> 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_REACH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ((en >> dma_chan) & 1) = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_DISABLED
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  cs = DmaRegRead(#DMA_CS)
  If (cs & #DMA_CS_ACTIVE) <> 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_BUSY
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If DmaChannelReset() = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_BUSY
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  dbg = DmaRegRead(#DMA_DEBUG)
  If (dbg & #DMA_DEBUG_LITE) <> 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_LITE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If dma_ybias < 0
    If DmaCalibrate() = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  dma_ready = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The raw ENABLE register, for a diagnostic to print. The PAGE and
; PAGELITE fields in the top byte are the interesting part and the
; reason this is exposed at all.
Procedure.i DmaEnableRaw()
  ProcedureReturn DmaGlobalRead(#DMA_ENABLE)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DmaScratchBase()
  ProcedureReturn dma_scratch
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  ARGUMENT CHECKING - one place, so the entry points stay readable
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaGeomOk - do these dimensions fit the register fields at all?
;
; XLENGTH is sixteen bits and YLENGTH is fourteen, so a row longer than
; 65535 bytes or a rectangle taller than 16383 rows cannot be expressed
; and would be TRUNCATED rather than refused if this did not check. A
; truncated XLENGTH is a fill of the wrong width, which on a
; framebuffer reads as a diagonal shear and gets blamed on the pitch -
; display.pi4:313-331 documents that exact misdiagnosis from the CPU
; side.
;
; The stride is a SIGNED sixteen-bit field, so the gap between one row
; and the next cannot exceed 32767 bytes. A 1920-pixel 32bpp surface
; has a pitch of 7680 and a gap of zero on a full-width fill, so this
; is generous today and will not be on a 4K surface: 3840 pixels is
; 15360 bytes a row, still inside XLENGTH, but a narrow column near the
; right edge of such a surface has a stride approaching 15360 and a
; 8K surface would exceed the field. Refused, not truncated.
;
; A PITCH SMALLER THAN THE ROW IS REFUSED rather than handled. It would
; mean either overlapping rows or a bottom-up surface with a negative
; stride, and both are legal for the hardware; neither has a caller
; here, and supporting a case with no caller is how the extent
; arithmetic in DmaDestOk() below acquires a sign bug nobody exercises.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaGeomOk(pitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i)
  If rowBytes < 1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rows < 1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rowBytes > #DMA_MAX_ROW_BYTES
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rows > #DMA_MAX_ROWS
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If pitch < rowBytes
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (pitch - rowBytes) > #DMA_STRIDE_MAX
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_STRIDE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The number of bytes a rectangle spans from its first byte to its
; last, inclusive - NOT rowBytes * rows, which is the area. The last
; row starts (rows-1)*pitch in and runs rowBytes long, and everything
; between the rows belongs to the surface too as far as "could this
; write escape the window" is concerned.
Procedure.i DmaRectSpan(pitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i)
  ProcedureReturn ((rows - 1) * pitch) + rowBytes
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaDestOk - is it safe to let the engine write here?
;
; Two separate questions and both have to be yes:
;
;   * IS IT INSIDE THE DECLARED WINDOW. HAZARD 3. The caller named one
;     region this file may write into and every byte of the rectangle
;     has to be in it.
;   * CAN THE ENGINE REACH IT. HAZARD 1. Inside the window is not the
;     same as inside the low gigabyte - a caller could declare a window
;     that DmaSetBounds() accepted and then hand over a rectangle at
;     the far end of it, and although DmaSetBounds() checks the whole
;     window this check costs nothing and does not depend on that.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaDestOk(dst.i, pitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i)
  Protected span.i
  Protected last.i

  If dma_haveBounds = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOBOUNDS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  span = DmaRectSpan(pitch, rowBytes, rows)
  last = (dst + span) - 1

  If dst < dma_bounds
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If last > ((dma_bounds + dma_boundsN) - 1)
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaAddrOk(dst, span) = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_REACH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE OPERATIONS
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaFillPattern32 - fill a rectangle with a repeating 32-bit word.
;
;   dst       byte address of the rectangle's first byte
;   pitch     bytes from the start of one row to the start of the next
;   rowBytes  bytes to write on each row
;   rows      how many rows
;   pattern   the 32-bit word, repeated
;
; BYTES, NOT PIXELS, on purpose. This is the primitive and it is below
; the graphics seam: it does not know what a pixel is, which is what
; lets DmaFill32() sit on top of it for 32bpp and lets a 16bpp caller
; use it by passing a doubled half-word and rowBytes = width * 2.
;
; HOW A COPY ENGINE FILLS. There is no fill mode. The engine is pointed
; at a 32-byte pattern in the scratch block with TI.SRC_INC CLEAR, so
; the source address never advances and it reads the same wide word for
; the whole transfer; TI.DEST_INC is set, so the destination walks.
; TI.SRC_IGNORE, which sounds like it should be involved, is not: it
; "zero all the write strobes" (:3303-3311) and exists for cache fills,
; so it would write nothing.
;
; THE CACHE, AND WHAT THE CALLER STILL OWES. This call cleans its own
; control block and its own pattern - HAZARD 2 - and does NOT touch the
; destination. If the CPU is going to READ these pixels afterwards it
; must invalidate them first, with DmaCacheRange(dst, span). If the
; only reader is the display controller, nothing more is needed: the
; engine wrote through to memory and the scan-out never looks at the
; ARM's caches.
;
; AND THE CALLER MUST NOT HAVE DIRTY LINES OVER THE DESTINATION. If the
; CPU drew into this rectangle earlier with the caches on and those
; lines are still dirty, they can be evicted AFTER the engine's writes
; land and overwrite them. That is a genuine hazard of mixing the two
; paths on one surface, it is invisible until it is not, and the fix is
; one DmaCacheRange() over the rectangle before the fill. It is the
; caller's because only the caller knows whether the CPU touched it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaFillPattern32(dst.i, pitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i, pattern.i)
  Protected ti.i
  Protected i.i
  Protected p.i
  Protected ylen.i
  Protected lenWord.i
  Protected strideWord.i

  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If dma_ready = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOTREADY
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaGeomOk(pitch, rowBytes, rows) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The fill's own row cap, separate from the copy's. It refuses
  ; nothing today - see the long note beside #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES -
  ; and it is wired in now so that if the gate's bracketing cases find
  ; a real limit on this part, turning it into a refusal is one
  ; constant and not a code change.
  If rowBytes > #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ROWLIMIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaDestOk(dst, pitch, rowBytes, rows) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Lay the pattern down - all 256 bytes of it, every time.
  ;
  ; PokeL, thirty-two bits at a time: a bare Poke would be eight bytes
  ; and would write two pattern words at once, which happens to be
  ; right here and would be wrong the moment anybody made the pattern
  ; something other than a repeated word.
  ;
  ; SIXTY-FOUR STORES PER FILL LOOKS LIKE WASTE AND IS NOT. It is a
  ; fixed cost of about two hundred instructions against a transfer
  ; that moves megabytes, it is four cache lines rather than one, and
  ; the alternative - remember the last colour and skip the loop when
  ; it has not changed - would leave the pattern buffer holding a
  ; value this file believes is there rather than one it just wrote.
  ; That is precisely the class of assumption the 2026-08-26 failure
  ; came out of, and it is not worth 200 instructions to reintroduce.
  p = dma_scratch + #DMA_SCRATCH_PATTERN
  i = 0
  While i < #DMA_PATTERN_BYTES
    PokeL(p + i, pattern & $FFFFFFFF)
    i = i + 4
  Wend

  ti = 0
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_TDMODE
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_SRC_WIDTH        ; 128-bit reads of the pattern
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_WIDTH       ; 128-bit writes
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_INC
  ti = ti | ((dma_burst & $F) << #DMA_TI_BURST_SHIFT)
  ; SRC_INC is deliberately CLEAR. That is the whole trick.

  ylen       = rows - dma_ybias
  lenWord    = ((ylen & $3FFF) << 16) | (rowBytes & $FFFF)
  strideWord = (((pitch - rowBytes) & $FFFF) << 16) | 0

  ProcedureReturn DmaSubmit(ti, DmaBusAddr(p), DmaBusAddr(dst), lenWord, strideWord)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaFill32 - the same thing in pixels, for a 32bpp surface.
;
; THE ONE TO COMPARE AGAINST THE CPU. display.pi4's DspRowFill32 is the
; loop this replaces, and its own header (display.pi4:2372-2410)
; records what it costs: a thirty-instruction body per pixel unrolled
; down to about 1.4 instructions per byte, which measured 266.4 ms over
; the full surface with the caches on.
;
;     DmaFill32(DisplayBase(), DisplayPitch(), DisplayWidth(),
;               DisplayHeight(), colour)
;
; is the same rectangle, and RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/
; pi4DmaProbe.pi4 times both in one run so the comparison is direct
; rather than across two sessions.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaFill32(dst.i, pitch.i, widthPixels.i, rows.i, colour.i)
  If widthPixels < 1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DmaFillPattern32(dst, pitch, widthPixels * 4, rows, colour & $FFFFFFFF)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaCopy2D - copy a rectangle from one surface to another.
;
;   dst, dstPitch   where it goes, and that surface's row spacing
;   src, srcPitch   where it comes from, and that surface's row spacing
;   rowBytes        how many bytes on each row
;   rows            how many rows
;
; This is the sprite blit, the shadow-buffer present, and the scroll.
; It is a straight copy - THERE IS NO BLEND AND THERE IS NO FORMAT
; CONVERSION. Both surfaces must already be in the same pixel format,
; which is the caller's business and not something a byte-shaped
; primitive can check.
;
; THE CALLER OWES TWO CACHE OPERATIONS AND THIS ONE DOES NEITHER:
;   * CLEAN THE SOURCE before the call, if the CPU wrote it -
;     DmaCacheRange(src, span). Without it the engine copies whatever
;     DRAM last saw, which on a freshly composed shadow buffer is
;     usually nothing at all.
;   * INVALIDATE THE DESTINATION after the call, if the CPU will read
;     it back.
; DmaFillPattern32's note on dirty lines over the destination applies
; here too, and for the same reason.
;
; OVERLAP. A copy whose destination starts ABOVE its source is refused,
; and one whose destination starts BELOW its source is allowed. The
; asymmetry is the engine's address order: SRC_INC and DEST_INC both
; increment (:3327-3374), so a row is copied low-to-high and the rows
; are visited in the order the strides walk them. A downward copy - the
; scroll a console does - therefore reads each byte before the write
; that would have clobbered it, and an upward copy does not.
;
;   THAT IS AN ASSUMPTION, AND IT IS MARKED AS ONE. The PDF states the
;   increments but does not state that reads and writes retire in
;   address order under bursting, and it does not have to for the
;   hardware to be correct. pi4DmaProbe.pi4 tests it explicitly with an
;   overlapping downward copy of a known pattern; if that test fails,
;   the fix is to refuse overlap in BOTH directions here and let the
;   caller stage through a temporary.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaCopy2D(dst.i, dstPitch.i, src.i, srcPitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i)
  Protected ti.i
  Protected ylen.i
  Protected lenWord.i
  Protected strideWord.i
  Protected dstSpan.i
  Protected srcSpan.i

  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If dma_ready = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOTREADY
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaGeomOk(dstPitch, rowBytes, rows) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaGeomOk(srcPitch, rowBytes, rows) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaDestOk(dst, dstPitch, rowBytes, rows) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  dstSpan = DmaRectSpan(dstPitch, rowBytes, rows)
  srcSpan = DmaRectSpan(srcPitch, rowBytes, rows)

  ; The SOURCE is not bounds-checked against the write window - it is a
  ; read, and a read cannot corrupt anything - but it IS reach-checked,
  ; because a source above the cap is the silent-failure case HAZARD 1
  ; exists for.
  If DmaAddrOk(src, srcSpan) = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_REACH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If dst < (src + srcSpan)
    If src < (dst + dstSpan)
      If dst > src
        dma_err = #DMA_ERR_OVERLAP
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ti = 0
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_TDMODE
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_SRC_WIDTH
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_SRC_INC
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_WIDTH
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_INC
  ti = ti | ((dma_burst & $F) << #DMA_TI_BURST_SHIFT)

  ylen       = rows - dma_ybias
  lenWord    = ((ylen & $3FFF) << 16) | (rowBytes & $FFFF)
  strideWord = (((dstPitch - rowBytes) & $FFFF) << 16) | ((srcPitch - rowBytes) & $FFFF)

  ProcedureReturn DmaSubmit(ti, DmaBusAddr(src), DmaBusAddr(dst), lenWord, strideWord)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DmaCopy - one contiguous run of bytes. LINEAR, not 2D.
;
; TDMODE clear, so TXFR_LEN is read as a single transfer of the full
; thirty-bit length {YLENGTH, XLENGTH} (:3391-3400) - up to a gigabyte
; in one control block, with no 65535-byte row limit and no stride
; arithmetic to get wrong.
;
; THIS IS THE PRESENT PATH and it is why the linear case is a separate
; entry point rather than "DmaCopy2D with one row". Pushing a whole
; shadow buffer to a framebuffer whose pitch equals width*bpp is one
; contiguous 8.29 MB run; expressing it as 1080 rows of 7680 bytes
; would ask the engine to re-evaluate a stride 1080 times for no
; reason. display.pi4's DspFlushSpan (:2316-2331) carves out the same
; special case on the cache side, for the same reason and with the same
; warning: this is ONLY correct when the region really is contiguous,
; which for a surface means whole rows AND pitch = width * bpp.
;
; The overlap rule is DmaCopy2D's.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DmaCopy(dst.i, src.i, bytes.i)
  Protected ti.i

  dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NONE

  If dma_ready = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_NOTREADY
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If bytes < 1
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The whole of TXFR_LEN is thirty bits when TDMODE is clear.
  If bytes > $3FFFFFFF
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_ARGS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaDestOk(dst, bytes, bytes, 1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DmaAddrOk(src, bytes) = 0
    dma_err = #DMA_ERR_REACH
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If dst < (src + bytes)
    If src < (dst + bytes)
      If dst > src
        dma_err = #DMA_ERR_OVERLAP
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ti = 0
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_SRC_WIDTH
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_SRC_INC
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_WIDTH
  ti = ti | #DMA_TI_DEST_INC
  ti = ti | ((dma_burst & $F) << #DMA_TI_BURST_SHIFT)
  ; TDMODE deliberately CLEAR, and the STRIDE word therefore unused.

  ProcedureReturn DmaSubmit(ti, DmaBusAddr(src), DmaBusAddr(dst), bytes & $3FFFFFFF, 0)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  ERRORS IN WORDS
;
;  Every message names the check that failed AND what to do about it,
;  because the audience is somebody at a bench with a board that is not
;  doing what they expected and no debugger.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i DmaErrorText()
  Select dma_err
    Case #DMA_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn "dma: ok"
    Case #DMA_ERR_NOSCRATCH
      ProcedureReturn "dma: no scratch block - call DmaSetScratch() with 512 bytes, 256-byte aligned, below $40000000"
    Case #DMA_ERR_ALIGN
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the scratch block is not 256-byte aligned. The control block needs 32 (CONBLK_AD is 256-BIT aligned) and the 256-byte fill pattern wants 256 so that one maximal read burst from its base cannot straddle a 4 KiB boundary"
    Case #DMA_ERR_SMALL
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the scratch block is shorter than 512 bytes - it holds a 32-byte control block, 224 bytes reserved for a chained block, and a 256-byte fill pattern. It was 128 until 2026-08-26, when a 32-byte pattern turned out to be smaller than one read burst and the engine filled the tail of a long row with whatever lay behind it"
    Case #DMA_ERR_REACH
      ProcedureReturn "dma: an address is at or above $40000000 - the legacy engines are thirty-bit and cannot see the rest of DRAM. This is NOT the $C0000000 PCIe cap; it is three quarters tighter. If this came from DmaInit(), the ENABLE register's PAGE field is non-zero and something else on this board has moved the whole one-gigabyte window"
    Case #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL
      ProcedureReturn "dma: not a channel this file may drive - 1 and 3 belong to the firmware per brcm,dma-channel-mask, 11 to 14 are DMA4 with a different control block, 15 is the VPU's. Use 0, 2, 4, 5 or 6"
    Case #DMA_ERR_LITE
      ProcedureReturn "dma: that is a LITE engine and LITE engines have no 2D mode at all - TDMODE, the strides and YLENGTH are simply absent, so a rectangle would become a linear transfer of tens of millions of bytes"
    Case #DMA_ERR_DISABLED
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the channel's bit in the global ENABLE register is clear, so it is powered down. The reset value is 1, so somebody cleared it deliberately; this file will not set it, because that register also warns that disabling a running channel is fatal"
    Case #DMA_ERR_BUSY
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the channel was already ACTIVE - somebody else is using it, or a previous transfer is still in flight. Nothing here will reset a channel out from under a live transfer"
    Case #DMA_ERR_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the transfer did not finish inside two seconds and the channel has been aborted and reset. Check the source and destination addresses first - a buffer above $40000000 produces a device that accepts everything and completes nothing, which is indistinguishable at the bench from a missing cache clean"
    Case #DMA_ERR_HW
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the engine reported an error - CS.ERROR, or one of READ_ERROR, FIFO_ERROR, READ_LAST_NOT_SET_ERROR in the DEBUG register. DmaLastDebug() has the raw value"
    Case #DMA_ERR_ARGS
      ProcedureReturn "dma: width, height or pitch out of range - a row cannot exceed 65535 bytes, a rectangle cannot exceed 16383 rows, and the pitch may not be smaller than the row"
    Case #DMA_ERR_STRIDE
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the gap between rows does not fit the signed sixteen-bit stride field, so it may not exceed 32767 bytes"
    Case #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the destination is outside the window DmaSetBounds() declared. This is the check that stops a runaway bus master writing over the monitor; widen the window deliberately or fix the rectangle"
    Case #DMA_ERR_NOBOUNDS
      ProcedureReturn "dma: DmaSetBounds() has never been called, and it is mandatory - this engine writes physical addresses with nothing in the path to stop it, so it is told once and explicitly where it may write"
    Case #DMA_ERR_NOTREADY
      ProcedureReturn "dma: DmaInit() has not run, or it refused. Call it and read DmaErrorText() again"
    Case #DMA_ERR_CALIB
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the YLENGTH calibration gave an answer that is neither convention. DmaCalibrateAdvance() has the source-pointer advance: 4 means a plain row count, 8 means rows minus one, 65540 means TDMODE was ignored and the transfer went linear, anything else means SOURCE_AD does not read back the way the datasheet says. DmaSetYLengthBias() is how to supply the answer by hand"
    Case #DMA_ERR_OVERLAP
      ProcedureReturn "dma: source and destination overlap with the destination above the source, which a forward copy would corrupt. A downward copy - the scroll case - is allowed"
    Case #DMA_ERR_BURST
      ProcedureReturn "dma: that burst length is at or above the one that locked the AXI read bus on this board on 2026-08-26 - the board hung and the deadman reset it. The cap is #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE and it is deliberately a refusal rather than a silent clamp. Only pi4DmaGate.pi4's group F may exceed it, through DmaSetBurstProbe(), and only with the deadman armed"
    Case #DMA_ERR_ROWLIMIT
      ProcedureReturn "dma: the fill row is longer than #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES. That constant is the largest row a fill on this part is known to complete correctly; if you are seeing this, somebody measured a hardware limit with the gate's group A2 and wrote it down. Split the rectangle, or use DmaCopy2D from a prepared row"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "dma: unknown error"
EndProcedure
